Ambiator, for scaling and commercialisation.","table":null},{"a":"Acceleration Initiative for Devices, Diagnostics and Digital Health","e":"Around 24 startups were supported through 60+ workshops, enabling 20+ hospital collaborations and raising Rs. 25+ crore in funding.","table":null},{"a":"Apollo Hospitals, Fernandez Hospital, NIMS","e":"","table":null},{"a":"40+ women students","e":"From Tier-2 and Tier-3 institutions, partnered with 9 institutions and 15 mentors.","table":null},{"a":"Rangareddy district","e":"The project aimed to enhance productivity and climate resilience for 1,000 farmers using data-driven agronomy.","table":null},{"a":"1,200+ MSMEs","e":"RICH also conducted Resource efficiency and Cleaner Production assessments covering more than 60 MSMEs.","table":null},{"a":"Automated Demand Response systems","e":"Implemented as part of the India-Norway Task Force on Energy, piloted with over 250 consumers, reducing up to 10% of energy bills.","table":null},{"a":"40 key interventions","e":"Organised into 6 key areas targeting access to land, finance, technology, raw materials, labour and markets.","table":null},{"a":"10% GSDP contribution increase, 15% annual registration growth","e":"The policy aims to increase MSMEs'' contribution to Telangana GSDP by 10% and achieve 15% annual growth in MSME registrations.","table":null},{"a":"3,47,931 registrations","e":"These span across 33 districts in Telangana.","table":null},{"a":"Hyderabad","e":"Hyderabad (56,768), Medchal-Malkajgiri (44,162), and Rangareddy (38,057) districts lead in registrations.","table":null},{"a":"Hyderabad highest","e":"","table":{"headers":["District","Units"],"rows":[["Hyderabad","56,768"],["Medchal-Malkajgiri","44,162"],["Rangareddy","38,057"],["Nalgonda","16,011"],["Nizamabad","13,525"],["Karimnagar","13,413"],["Sangareddy","12,844"],["Yadadri Bhuvanagiri","10,790"],["Khammam","9,939"],["Jagtial","9,067"],["Siddipet","8,793"],["Mahabubnagar","8,165"]],"unit":"Number of units registered, July 2020 to 22 December 2025"}},{"a":"219 programmes, 18,772 MSMEs/SHG members","e":"","table":null},{"a":"134 programmes","e":"Benefiting 3,488 SHG members transitioning into MSMEs.","table":null},{"a":"39 MSMEs, Rs. 7.62 crore","e":"","table":null},{"a":"1,375 new women entrepreneurs","e":"Growth support was also provided to 153 existing women-led enterprises.","table":null},{"a":"73 MSMEs, Rs. 73.10 lakh","e":"","table":null},{"a":"109 startups incubated, 9 received seed funding","e":"The 9 startups received Rs. 65 lakh in seed funding through IIT Hyderabad and ALEAP.","table":null},{"a":"8,947 MSMEs","e":"Comprising 8,888 Bronze, 28 Silver, and 31 Gold certifications.","table":null},{"a":"Central Sector Scheme for artisans, 18 trades","e":"It provides comprehensive support to traditional artisans and craftspeople.","table":null},{"a":"3,57,256 applications received","e":"","table":{"headers":["Metric","Number"],"rows":[["Applications received","3,57,256"],["Artisans registered","89,036"],["Beneficiaries completing training","64,869"],["Loans sanctioned","28,631"],["Loans disbursed","26,789"],["Toolkits distributed","28,269"]],"unit":"Count, cumulative under PM Vishwakarma Yojana"}},{"a":"Rs. 246.42 crore","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Hyderabad (MSME Tool Room)","e":"With an extension centre at Karimnagar; a new Technology Centre in Warangal district is under consideration.","table":null},{"a":"SAFRAN-CFM International Aero Engine MRO facility","e":"Located in Hyderabad, it is the first and largest aero engine MRO facility established by any OEM in India, inaugurated by the Prime Minister of India.","table":null},{"a":"M88 military engine MRO facility","e":"Used in Rafale aircraft; groundbreaking was undertaken by the Chief Minister of Telangana.","table":null},{"a":"Skyroot Aerospace","e":"Based in Hyderabad, inaugurated by the Prime Minister of India.","table":null},{"a":"Pratt & Whitney, Safran, Dassault Aviation","e":"TASL is manufacturing aero engine components for Pratt & Whitney and Safran, and aerostructures for Dassault Aviation''s Rafale aircraft.","table":null},{"a":"Aerospace and Defence","e":"This marks a major economic milestone reflecting rapid scaling of high-value manufacturing.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 20,000 crore","e":"Major commitments came from GMR Group, ITP Aero (Spain), Solar Industries, and Apollo Microsystems.","table":null},{"a":"Paris Air Show, Aerospace Valley","e":"Telangana secured two speaking slots at the Paris Air Show and signed an MoU with Aerospace Valley, France''s largest aerospace and defence cluster.","table":null},{"a":"Aero India (Bengaluru), Aeromart Nagoya (Japan)","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Aircraft MRO and Shipbuilding MRO programmes","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Tata Technologies","e":"","table":null},{"a":"WINGS 2026","e":"Held 28-30 January 2026, it is India''s largest civil aviation airshow.","table":null},{"a":"Best State Award","e":"From the Ministry of Civil Aviation, in the category of Best State for Promotion of Aviation Ecosystem.","table":null},{"a":"Pochampally Ikats and others","e":"Also includes Gadwal Cotton, Silk and Sico Sarees, Narayanpet Cotton and Silk Sarees, Warangal Durries, and Karimnagar Bedsheets and Furnishings.","table":null},{"a":"23,930 Handlooms, 43,893 Powerlooms","e":"","table":null},{"a":"8% weaver contribution, 16% government contribution","e":"Under the \"Telangana Chenetha Abhayahastha Padakam,\" each weaver contributes 8% of wages monthly while the Government contributes double, i.e. 16%.","table":null},{"a":"34,482 weavers and ancillary workers","e":"With financial expenditure of Rs. 13.56 crore.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 5.00 lakh","e":"Paid to the nominee of a deceased weaver/ancillary worker in the event of death due to any cause.","table":null},{"a":"48,670 weavers/ancillary workers","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 18,000 per weaver, Rs. 6,000 per ancillary worker","e":"Support is based on the number of Telangana Authentic Weaves Labels issued to each geo-tagged loom.","table":null},{"a":"32,788 members","e":"Comprising 17,028 weavers and 15,760 ancillary workers.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 1.00 lakh per weaver","e":"Covers outstanding and closed loans originating from April 1, 2017, to March 31, 2024.","table":null},{"a":"6,784 beneficiaries, Rs. 49.27 crore","e":"Comprising 5,192 Outstanding Loans and 1,592 Closed Loans across 21 districts.","table":null},{"a":"Interest burden on Handloom Weavers Cooperative Societies","e":"In 2024-25, the department supported 60 societies with a financial outlay of Rs. 1.09 crore.","table":null},{"a":"One NHE, one SHE","e":"","table":null},{"a":"35 clusters","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Kosigi, Ieeza, Yadagirigutta, Choutuppal","e":"Proposed with an outlay of Rs. 4.05 crore.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 5.39 crore","e":"Of which Rs. 1.35 crore has been released by the Government.","table":null},{"a":"Awards for excelling handloom weavers/designers","e":"Given on National Handloom Day (7th August every year); 33 awardees were felicitated in 2025.","table":null},{"a":"TGERC recommendation dated 28.10.2024","e":"Power tariff for Cottage Industry Category-IV(A) enhanced from 10 HP to up to 25 HP; approximately 9,875 powerloom units benefit.","table":null},{"a":"15,000 beneficiaries, Rs. 30.00 crore budget","e":"In 2024-25, it achieved 11,698 beneficiaries with Rs. 34.07 crore financial achievement.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 724.33 crore","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Distribute One Crore sarees to One Crore Women","e":"So far 89.60 lakh sarees have been supplied to SERP and MEPMA for distribution.","table":null},{"a":"Buffer stock depot, Rs. 50.00 crore corpus","e":"Established in October 2024 with TGSCO as nodal agency; 2,655 metric tonnes of yarn supplied so far.","table":null},{"a":"Diploma in Handlooms and Textiles Technology (DHTT)","e":"Established in 2024-25, it admits 60 students annually.","table":null},{"a":"Units grew from 1,065 to 1,292","e":"","table":null},{"a":"GOLKONDA","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Four CFCs","e":"At Bonthapally (wooden toys), Karimnagar (Silver Filigree), Devarkonda (Banjara Embroidery), and Nirmal (Nirmal Toys).","table":null},{"a":"18 GI-tagged products","e":"Include Pochampally Ikat, Hyderabad Haleem, Nirmal Toys and Crafts, Warangal Durries, among others.","table":null},{"a":"6 additional applications","e":"Include Banjara Needle Craft, Banjara Tribal Jewellery, Narayanpet Jewellery Making, Hyderabad Pearls, Kothakota Mutton, and Batik Paintings.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 63.00 crore","e":"","table":null},{"a":"10 State Awards","e":"3 Awards of Rs. 50,000/40,000/35,000 and 7 Awards of Rs. 20,000 each, totalling Rs. 2.65 lakh expenditure.","table":null},{"a":"Promote the leather industry","e":"Includes skill upgradation, training for leather artisans, and managing marketing activities.","table":null},{"a":"Mini Leather Park in 25 acres at Armoor, Nizamabad","e":"Will eventually house 120 plots with total investment of Rs. 53 crore, creating direct employment for 1,000 people.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 9.37 crore total","e":"State Government contributed Rs. 2.61 crore, Central Government contributed Rs. 6.76 crore.","table":null},{"a":"Rukmapur (Karimnagar), Station Ghanpur (Janagaon)","e":"Proposed under the Indian Footwear and Leather Development Programme (IFLDP) scheme.","table":null},{"a":"87 acres at Rukmapur, 120 acres at Station Ghanpur","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Footwear Design and Development Institute (FDDI)","e":"To train SC/ST and other candidates in manufacturing bags, garments, footwear, and goods.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 73,360 crore, 190+ investments","e":"This surge generated over 1,00,000 direct jobs and 2,50,000 indirect jobs.","table":null},{"a":"5,00,000 new jobs, USD 25 billion investments","e":"The policy aims to establish Telangana as one of the top five global life sciences clusters by 2030.","table":null},{"a":"Eli Lilly","e":"The 2.2 lakh sq. ft. GCC created 1,500 jobs; Eli Lilly further announced a Rs. 8,850 crore investment for global manufacturing.","table":null},{"a":"HCA Healthcare, Johnson & Johnson, Rx Benefits","e":"HCA Healthcare invested Rs. 663.75 crore; Rx Benefits created over 300 jobs.","table":null},{"a":"20 lakh sq. ft. integrated R&D facility","e":"","table":null},{"a":"India''s first logical single-use bioprocess design-and-scale-up facility","e":"The Government of Telangana inaugurated this facility as part of its life sciences innovation push.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 12,610 crore, 10 pharmaceutical companies","e":"","table":null},{"a":"USD 2 billion to USD 10 billion","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Approximately 300 acres","e":"This will establish a Bio-Innovation and Bio-Manufacturing Cluster with multi-tenant laboratories.","table":null},{"a":"10 Pharma Villages","e":"Each spanning approximately 1,000 to 3,000 acres along the Outer Ring Road (ORR).","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 100 crore, scalable to Rs. 1,000 crore","e":"Scalable via PPP with co-investment alongside venture capital and private equity funds.","table":null},{"a":"\"TechBio Unleashed: AI, Automation & the Biology Revolution\"","e":"Held 16-18 February 2026 at HITEX Hyderabad, the 23rd edition of Asia''s premier life sciences forum.","table":null},{"a":"4,500+ delegates, 120+ companies","e":"Also featured 40 startups and facilitated over 4,000 B2B meetings.","table":null},{"a":"Novartis","e":"","table":null},{"a":"ECity, Maheshwaram, Divitipally, Future City","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Strategic implementation arm under ITE&C Department","e":"Established to make Telangana a global hub for semiconductors, photonics, VLSI, IoT, quantum technologies, and advanced electronics.","table":null},{"a":"USD 2.25 billion","e":"With a USD 400 million first phase; has received a 29% State incentive package and seeks a 50% capital subsidy from the Government of India.","table":null},{"a":"Over 1,000 high-value direct jobs","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Telangana Semiconductor and Hardware Innovation for Next-Generation Employability","e":"A VLSI skilling and placement framework in collaboration with MOSart Labs and NIELIT.","table":null},{"a":"Telangana Advanced Characterisation and Electronics Test Lab","e":"Focuses on VLSI innovation, characterisation, and product validation, partnering with MOSart Labs.","table":null},{"a":"100% exemption on road tax and registration fees","e":"","table":null},{"a":"2 lakh registrations","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Over 1,000 stations","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Japan (Tokyo and Hiroshima)","e":"High-level interactions were held with automotive OEMs, component manufacturers, and mobility technology firms.","table":null},{"a":"Drive to Zero Summit, Policymakers'' Knowledge Exchange Programme","e":"Drive to Zero Summit was organised by CALSTART in the Netherlands; the Knowledge Exchange Programme covered Norway, Netherlands, and Belgium.","table":null},{"a":"State''s Nodal Agency for food processing","e":"Implements State and Central Government schemes related to food processing.","table":null},{"a":"14 SFPZs, 7,150 acres","e":"This provides plug-and-play infrastructure and drives 70% high-value tertiary processing.","table":null},{"a":"176 applications, Rs. 7,164.39 crore","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 5,068.36 crore, over 7,615 jobs","e":"","table":null},{"a":"743 units","e":"Attracted Rs. 1,928.3 crore of investment and created 10,335 new jobs.","table":null},{"a":"9,160 enterprises","e":"","table":{"headers":["Metric","Value"],"rows":[["Total enterprises","9,160"],["Fixed capital investment","Rs. 18,224.04 crore"],["Employment generated","1,31,959 personnel"]],"unit":"Cumulative since inception, as of 2025-26"}},{"a":"Impact Harvest Forum 2025","e":"Held 3-4 July 2025 in Hyderabad, focused on blended finance and inclusive business models for SHGs and FPOs.","table":null},{"a":"World Food India 2025","e":"Held 25-28 September 2025, showcasing processed-food value chains via micro food processing units.","table":null},{"a":"Food/beverage trends platform at HITEX, Hyderabad","e":"Held 14-16 November 2025, featured 150+ exhibitors and 5,000+ business visitors.","table":null},{"a":"Vision 2047 investment platform, 8-9 December 2025","e":"Held at Bharat Future City, Hyderabad; secured MoUs with anchor processors for Dairy, Beverages, FMCG, Fisheries, and Agri-processing.","table":null},{"a":"Telangana Trade Promotion Corporation Limited","e":"Designated as the Nodal Agency for logistics infrastructure development in the State.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 202 crore interest-free loan","e":"Sanctioned by the Government of India under the SASCI scheme; being constructed in Raidurg, proposed for completion by 2029.","table":null},{"a":"500 direct, 1,000 indirect","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 2,049.35 crore","e":"Located at Manoharabad Mandal, Medak District, on 315-01 acres-guntas under a Greenfield project.","table":null},{"a":"More than 1,500 direct and indirect jobs","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 20.22 crore budget outlay","e":"Located in Rangareddy District, under joint venture with GMR Air Cargo & Aerospace Engineering Ltd. (GACAEL), enhances food safety via controlled ionizing radiation.","table":null},{"a":"Coal","e":"Telangana is the only State in Southern India with vast coal deposits, mined by Singareni Collieries Company Ltd.","table":null},{"a":"7,519 MT (Million Tonnes)","e":"Found in Adilabad, Mancherial, Peddapally, Nalgonda, Suryapet, and Vikarabad districts, feeding 21 cement plants with 29.50 MTPA combined capacity.","table":null},{"a":"2,552 leases","e":"Covering over 72,516.57 hectares; nearly 90% fall under the small sector.","table":null},{"a":"Granite cutting & Polishing Units highest","e":"","table":{"headers":["Mineral-Based Industry","Number of Industries"],"rows":[["Granite cutting & Polishing Units","723"],["Stone Crushing Units","463"],["Brick Klins","234"],["Napaslab Units","183"],["Ready Mix Concrete Units","125"],["Quartz Pulverising Units","79"],["Manufacture Sand Units","74"],["Fuller''s Earth Processing Units","55"],["Clay & Ceramic Units","29"],["Cement Plants","21"],["Mosaic Chips Units","16"],["Sponge Iron Plants","15"],["Thermal Power Plants","3"],["Ferro Alloys Units","2"],["Laterite Beneficiation Plants","2"],["Barytes Processing Units","1"]],"unit":"Number of industries, Source: Department of Mines and Geology"}},{"a":"Rs. 5,517.60 crore (2024-25), Rs. 3,205.30 crore (2025-26)","e":"","table":null},{"a":"20%, effective July 8, 2025","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 6,880.44 crore","e":"A non-profit body mandated by the Ministry of Mines, incorporating PMKKKY welfare provisions for mining-affected areas.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 198.55 crore","e":"Funded by a 2% seigniorage fee contribution from leaseholders; conducts exploration of 31 minor minerals.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 1,000 crore target, Rs. 669.85 crore achieved","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Approximately 15,000 families","e":"Sand Mining also supports 387 outsourcing employees at the field level.","table":null},{"a":"32,66,304 MT","e":"Generating Rs. 6,53,26,080 in Escot charges; it has held a 5-star Ministry of Mines rating for 3 consecutive years.","table":null},{"a":"Approximately 18.40 lakh metric tonnes","e":"","table":null},{"a":"1.98 lakh metric tonnes, 10.73% recovery rate","e":"From 18.33 lakh metric tonnes of cane crushed by 7 operational mills.","table":null},{"a":"8.25 crore litres","e":"From an installed distillery capacity of 336 Kilo Litres Per Day; 1.29 crore litres supplied to OMCs for blending.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 4/- per MT cane crushed","e":"Contributed by both the Cane Farmer and Factory.","table":null},{"a":"7th rank","e":"With exports reaching Rs. 1,61,843 crore, a 39.30% increase from Rs. 1,16,182 crore in 2023-24.","table":null},{"a":"Gujarat highest","e":"","table":{"headers":["State","Share of Total Merchandise Exports (%)"],"rows":[["Gujarat","26.56"],["Maharashtra","15.05"],["Tamil Nadu","11.92"],["Karnataka","6.98"],["Uttar Pradesh","5.03"],["Andhra Pradesh","4.75"],["Telangana","4.37"],["West Bengal","2.90"],["Odisha","2.31"],["Madhya Pradesh","1.79"]],"unit":"%, April 2024 to March 2025"}},{"a":"8th nationally, 2nd among landlocked States","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Aircraft/Spacecraft top sector, Rangareddy top district","e":"","table":{"headers":["Top 5 Sectors","Value (Rs. crore)","Share (%)"],"rows":[["Aircraft, Spacecraft & Parts","47,577","29.40"],["Pharmaceutical Products","43,461","26.85"],["Organic Chemicals","24,868","15.37"],["Electrical Machinery & Equip.","8,081","4.99"],["Boilers, Machinery, Mechanical Appliances, and Parts","6,551","4.05"]]}},{"a":"90.10%","e":"","table":null},{"a":"USA highest","e":"","table":{"headers":["Country","Share (%)"],"rows":[["USA","23.64"],["UAE","11.74"],["Saudi Arabia","6.74"],["China","3.69"],["France","3.38"],["UK","2.72"],["Turkey","2.32"],["Czech Republic","1.95"],["Bangladesh","1.83"],["Kuwait","1.75"]],"unit":"%, April 2024 to March 2025"}},{"a":"About 95 commodities, 200+ countries","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Districts as Export Hubs (DEH), One District One Product (ODOP)","e":"District Level Export Promotion Committees (DLEPCs) prepare District Export Action Plans (DEAP) in coordination with local DGFT (RA).","table":null},{"a":"By 2027","e":"Integrating global OEMs and 1,500+ MSMEs to lead in aircraft manufacturing and green aerospace innovation.","table":null},{"a":"At least 3% of output","e":"Key projects include India''s first Functional Food Park for nutraceuticals and the National Centre for Food Innovation (NCFI).","table":null},{"a":"Flatted factory complexes in each of the 119 constituencies","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Zero Illegal Mining, Zero Illegal Transportation, Zero Overloading","e":"To be achieved through a digital overhaul deploying AI/ML and drones for smart mining management.","table":null}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET subject_id=excluded.subject_id, chapter_number=excluded.chapter_number, title=excluded.title, answers_json=excluded.answers_json;
INSERT INTO chapter_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, chapter_number, title, answers_json) VALUES ('telangana-economy-chapter-6-services', 'telangana-economy-en', 6, 'Services', '[{"a":"68.6%","e":"This makes services the dominant pillar of Telangana''s economy, valued at Rs. 11.31 lakh crore.","table":null},{"a":"13.5%","e":"This is the highest sectoral growth rate, outperforming both the primary and secondary sectors.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 9,96,293 crore to Rs. 11,30,596 crore","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 16,48,613 crore","e":"The services sector''s Rs. 11,30,596 crore represents 68.6% of this total.","table":null},{"a":"36.3%","e":"This exceeds the all-India average of 32.8%.","table":null},{"a":"64.9%","e":"This compares to India''s urban services employment share of 61.9%.","table":null},{"a":"19.7% vs 20.6%","e":"Telangana''s rural services employment is slightly below the national average.","table":null},{"a":"41.9% males, 27.3% females","e":"Both figures are higher than the corresponding all-India averages.","table":null},{"a":"25.5% male, 12.2% female","e":"Telangana''s rural male participation exceeds the all-India level of 24.7%.","table":null},{"a":"65.1% male, 64.5% female","e":"Compared to 60.9% male and 64.9% female at the all-India level.","table":null},{"a":"36.5% male, 24.8% female","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Telangana, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu","e":"","table":null},{"a":"About 7%","e":"This is as of the first half of calendar year 2025 (H1 CY2025).","table":null},{"a":"4th rank","e":"Behind Karnataka (39%), Maharashtra (14%) and Delhi (9%).","table":null},{"a":"USD 1.95 trillion","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Core Urban Region Economy","e":"Identified within the 160 km Outer Ring Road (ORR) as a net-zero, services-focused metropolis.","table":null},{"a":"Telangana AI Hub and 300-acre AI City","e":"The AI City will be located within Bharat Future City.","table":null},{"a":"Over 10 million citizens","e":"","table":null},{"a":"27 Special Tourism Areas","e":"","table":null},{"a":"17,22,031 MSMEs","e":"","table":null},{"a":"79.8%, 13,74,100 units","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Hyderabad","e":"Hyderabad has 2,38,879 units, followed by Ranga Reddy and Medchal-Malkajgiri.","table":null},{"a":"Hyderabad highest","e":"","table":{"headers":["District","Units"],"rows":[["Hyderabad","2,38,879"],["Rangareddy","1,63,776"],["Medchal-Malkajgiri","1,21,568"],["Nalgonda","64,723"],["Nizamabad","58,302"],["Karimnagar","51,875"],["Khammam","49,164"],["Sangareddy","46,594"],["Siddipet","38,249"],["Suryapet","38,113"],["Mahabubnagar","38,036"],["Hanumakonda","37,362"],["Warangal","36,415"],["Bhadradri Kothagudem","29,060"],["Mancherial","29,038"],["Jagtial","27,245"],["Yadadri Bhuvanagiri","25,469"],["Kamareddy","25,331"],["Vikarabad","23,914"],["Medak","23,829"],["Peddapalli","23,490"],["Adilabad","22,740"],["Nirmal","20,752"],["Nagarkurnool","20,741"],["Mahabubabad","20,471"],["Rajanna Sircilla","19,860"],["Wanaparthy","16,122"],["Jangaon","15,060"],["Jogulamba Gadwal","12,521"],["Kumuram Bheem","11,082"],["Jayashankar","9,446"],["Narayanpet","9,358"],["Mulugu","5,515"]],"unit":"Number of MSME units, Source: Department of Industries and Commerce, Telangana"}},{"a":"Mulugu, Jayashankar Bhupalapally, Narayanpet, Kumuram Bheem Asifabad, Jogulamba Gadwal","e":"These are predominantly rural and tribal districts, indicating uneven spatial distribution of enterprise activity.","table":null},{"a":"Real Estate, Ownership of Dwelling and Professional Services","e":"This sub-sector contributes Rs. 3,86,043 crore, accounting for 23.4% of total GSVA.","table":null},{"a":"Real Estate & Professional Services highest","e":"","table":{"headers":["Sub-Sector","Value (Rs. crore)","Share of Total GSVA (%)"],"rows":[["Real Estate, Ownership of Dwelling and Professional Services","3,86,043","23.4"],["Trade, Repair, Hotels and Restaurants","3,43,249","20.8"],["Transport, Storage, Communication & Services related to Broadcasting","1,38,257","8.4"],["Other Services","1,41,498","8.6"],["Financial Services","82,088","5.0"],["Public Administration","39,463","2.4"]],"unit":"Rs. crore and %, 2025-26 (PAE) at Current Prices"}},{"a":"Trade, Repair, Hotels and Restaurants","e":"This sub-sector contributed Rs. 3,43,249 crore, accounting for 20.8% of total GSVA.","table":null},{"a":"Trade, Repair, Hotels and Restaurants","e":"This sub-sector recorded a growth rate of 16.8%.","table":null},{"a":"Trade, Repair, Hotels highest","e":"","table":{"headers":["Sub-Sector","Growth Rate (%)"],"rows":[["Trade, Repair, Hotels and Restaurants","16.8"],["Transport, Storage, Communication & Services related to Broadcasting","14.1"],["Other Services","13.1"],["Financial Services","12.2"],["Real Estate, Ownership of Dwelling and Professional Services","11.7"],["Public Administration","5.9"]],"unit":"%, 2025-26 (PAE) at Current Prices"}},{"a":"Public Administration","e":"Public Administration grew at a relatively moderate pace of 5.9%.","table":null},{"a":"Over 1,500 companies","e":"Ranging from large multinational corporations to small and medium enterprises.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 3,12,941 crore","e":"This recorded a growth of 16.6% compared to 2023-24.","table":null},{"a":"9,39,054","e":"This underscores the sector''s significant contribution to the State''s economy.","table":null},{"a":"Offshore unit delivering specialised services to parent organisation","e":"GCCs support functions like IT, R&D, customer support, and business operations for their parent firms.","table":null},{"a":"Over 1,700 GCCs, USD 64.6 billion","e":"These GCCs employ 1.9 million professionals across hubs like Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Pune, Chennai, Mumbai, and NCR.","table":null},{"a":"Nearly 20%","e":"This makes Hyderabad India''s premier hub for Global Capability Centres.","table":null},{"a":"40 new GCCs","e":"This was the highest number among Indian cities.","table":null},{"a":"Vanguard, Eli Lilly, ZS, and others","e":"Also includes Medtronic IT Services, Amgen, Marriott, Citizens Financial Group, Olympus, Zoetis, and Dai-ichi Life Group.","table":null},{"a":"London Stock Exchange Group, Uber, and others","e":"Also includes Cognizant, Experian, Micron Technology, Sanofi, Medtronic, Providence, CDK Global, and Arcesium.","table":null},{"a":"USD 105 billion","e":"With approximately 2,400 GCCs employing over 2.8 million people.","table":null},{"a":"India''s most innovation-intensive GCC ecosystem","e":"Building on strengths in IT, pharmaceuticals, and governance while expanding into AI, life sciences, and sustainable technologies.","table":null},{"a":"GCC Policy (2025-2030)","e":"","table":null},{"a":"15 million sq. ft.","e":"This will anchor GCC clusters in Warangal, Karimnagar and Nizamabad.","table":null},{"a":"\"Asia''s Innovation Capital\"","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Around 30,000 professionals","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Animation, Visual Effects, Gaming and Comics","e":"It is a key component of the Orange Economy.","table":null},{"a":"Nearly two million professionals","e":"Employment potential in Telangana''s AVGC verticals is expected to scale beyond one lakh.","table":null},{"a":"IMAGE Tower","e":"Being developed at Raidurgam Village in Ranga Reddy District under a Joint Development/PPP model.","table":null},{"a":"Telangana Innovation Cell, T-Hub, Women Entrepreneurship Hub","e":"","table":null},{"a":"State Government''s first-response platform for grassroots innovators","e":"It serves start-ups, entrepreneurs, and innovation enablers, embedding a culture of innovation within governance and society.","table":null},{"a":"700 beneficiaries","e":"Products were developed by four start-ups and procured publicly for the PwD community.","table":null},{"a":"33 District Host Institutions","e":"One in each district, anchored by local higher education institutions to mentor grassroots entrepreneurs.","table":null},{"a":"28 innovators","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Tata Institute of Social Sciences (TISS)","e":"The review involved consultations with 73 incubators, 100 innovators, and over 100 entrepreneurs.","table":null},{"a":"More than 60 individuals","e":"Support ranged from ideation to proof of concept (PoC); needs assessments were also conducted for 16 innovators across 33 districts.","table":null},{"a":"Jayashankar Bhupalpally district","e":"Specifically in Mahadevpur Mandal.","table":null},{"a":"10 schools, around 2,000 girls","e":"Deployed across Hyderabad and Ranga Reddy districts, preventing nearly 1.5 lakh sanitary pads from reaching landfills annually.","table":null},{"a":"World''s largest start-up facility","e":"It is also India''s most advanced public-private innovation ecosystem, integrating start-ups, corporates, investors, academia, and Government.","table":null},{"a":"Aerospace and defence, sustainability, mobility, healthcare, AI","e":"","table":null},{"a":"World''s first Google for Startups Hub","e":"Located within an incubator, providing founders access to Google''s AI capabilities, cloud infrastructure, and global mentor network.","table":null},{"a":"Advancing SDG 5 (Gender Equality)","e":"It promotes women''s economic empowerment through the Triple Bottom Line of People, Planet, and Profit.","table":null},{"a":"197 programmes","e":"Including 80 bootcamps, achieving outreach across all 33 districts.","table":null},{"a":"Over 700 women entrepreneurs","e":"","table":null},{"a":"3,075 consultations, 6,701 EDP participants","e":"EDP stands for Entrepreneurship Development Programme.","table":null},{"a":"117 women-led enterprises","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Not-for-profit organisation for industry-ready human capital","e":"It creates synergy among Government institutions, industry, and academia, partnering with corporates on CSR-funded youth skilling initiatives.","table":null},{"a":"66,333 students, 4,627 faculty members","e":"","table":null},{"a":"2,520 trained, 2,226 certified and placed","e":"ESDM stands for Electronic System Design and Manufacturing; candidates were from Tier-II and Tier-III cities.","table":null},{"a":"5,637 placements","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 18 LPA highest (CISCO), Rs. 2.5 LPA average","e":"","table":null},{"a":"288 job drives, 25 districts, 101 colleges","e":"","table":null},{"a":"14,799 applicants, 1,378 final selections","e":"","table":null},{"a":"IBM Hackathon in collaboration with SmartBridge","e":"Held on 19th June 2025, where TASK students secured the top two positions.","table":null},{"a":"Live pitch event, 150+ entrepreneurs","e":"Shortlisted from more than 750 submissions, including i4TY members and rural innovators.","table":null},{"a":"24-hour hackathon with NxtWave Institute of Advanced Technologies (NIAT)","e":"180 teams and 600 students participated.","table":null},{"a":"ITE&C Department","e":"PVC strengthens skilling and innovation for semiconductors, photonics, VLSI, IoT, Quantum Technologies, and Advanced Electronics.","table":null},{"a":"\"Teams and Skill Sets for Advanced Technology Product Development\"","e":"Led by Silicon Labs.","table":null},{"a":"\"Advanced Automation Systems and Semiconductor Foundations\"","e":"With participation from AMD, Synopsys, and Silicon Labs.","table":null},{"a":"Over 600 students and faculty","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Telangana Skills, Academics and Training","e":"A digital education platform delivering learning via satellite, digital, and OTT platforms such as YuppTV, JioTV, and TSAT.TV.","table":null},{"a":"T-Fiber","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Over 22,400 videos, over 4.8 million subscribers","e":"","table":null},{"a":"1.5 million watch hours","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Arogya Mitra, Mano Mitra, Rythu Mitra, Spoorthi","e":"These provide continuous farmer-focused content on agriculture, rights, and innovation.","table":null},{"a":"Telangana State-Wide Students Annual Competitions 2025 (Season 1)","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Building emerging tech ecosystem and mainstreaming advanced tech adoption","e":"This covers both industry ecosystem development and Government department technology adoption.","table":null},{"a":"6 initiatives, 30+ AI pilots, 4 national awards","e":"Five additional programmes are also in the pipeline.","table":null},{"a":"J-PAL","e":"This partnership worked with 15 State departments to identify 18 AI-based use cases.","table":null},{"a":"Wadhwani Foundation","e":"The programme trained over 250 Government officers from more than 70 departments.","table":null},{"a":"Over 50 use cases","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Deakin University, Australia","e":"The collaboration aims to advance AI-enabled public sector solutions in health, education, and governance.","table":null},{"a":"Top 20 global AI hubs by 2035","e":"TAIH is a unified, autonomous institutional platform for artificial intelligence.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 250-300 crore, Section 8 company","e":"Investment planned over the first three years, under a triple-helix governance model of Government, academia, and industry/investors.","table":null},{"a":"Telangana AI Mission, Cybersecurity Centre of Excellence, TGDeX","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Telangana Data Exchange, launched July 2025","e":"It is India''s first State-led Digital Public Infrastructure (DPI) for Artificial Intelligence.","table":null},{"a":"Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA)","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Over 1,000 datasets, 25+ departments","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Over 2 lakh interactions, 350+ AI/ML models","e":"TGDeX also offers 10 departmental use-cases.","table":null},{"a":"3,000+ visitors, approx. 500 registered users, 820 dataset downloads","e":"","table":null},{"a":"420+ applications","e":"Launched under TGDeX to identify AI-based solutions for six priority Government use-cases.","table":null},{"a":"Education, Health, Transport, MSME, Revenue","e":"Note: source lists Education, Health, Transport, MSME, and Revenue departments (five named domains for six use-cases).","table":null},{"a":"AI-Powered Medical Imaging Diagnostics, and others","e":"Also includes AI-Powered High-Risk Pregnancy Prediction & Intervention, AI Chatbot for MSME Scheme Guidance, Optimising Public Transport Utilisation with AI, AI-Based Interactive Vocabulary Learning, and AI-Powered Document Verification for Property Registration.","table":null},{"a":"4 December 2025","e":"","table":null},{"a":"India''s National Quantum Mission (2023)","e":"","table":null},{"a":"BFSI, Defence, Life Sciences, Citizen Services, Cybersecurity","e":"BFSI stands for Banking, Financial Services, and Insurance.","table":null},{"a":"India''s first State-level quantum framework","e":"It strategises to build a complete quantum value chain from research to commercialisation.","table":null},{"a":"Research/Innovation, Skilling, Startups, Sectoral Adoption, Cybersecurity, Global Positioning","e":"","table":{"headers":["Pillar Number","Pillar Name"],"rows":[["1","Research, Innovation and Commercialisation"],["2","Skilling and Workforce Development"],["3","Startup Ecosystem Enablement"],["4","Sectoral Adoption"],["5","Cybersecurity and Resilience"],["6","Global Positioning and Partnerships"]],"unit":"Six core pillars of Telangana Quantum Strategy"}},{"a":"IIITH (CQST), IIT Hyderabad, TIFR, BITS Pilani Hyderabad, CR Rao AIMSCS, University of Hyderabad","e":"Supported by a flagship AI-Quantum Hub. CQST stands for Centre for Quantum Science and Technology.","table":null},{"a":"Advisory Council on Quantum Technologies","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Spatially disperse IT-led development beyond the western corridor","e":"This facilitates establishment of IT and ITeS units in clusters across Hyderabad''s other regions.","table":null},{"a":"Kandlakoya and Malakpet","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Airport and Shamshabad areas","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Uppal and Pocharam","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Telangana Fiber Grid Corporation Ltd.","e":"A cornerstone of the State''s digital infrastructure strategy, delivering robust, affordable, and scalable broadband connectivity.","table":null},{"a":"589 Mandals, 12,751 Gram Panchayats","e":"","table":null},{"a":"424 Mandals, 8,895 Gram Panchayats","e":"","table":null},{"a":"2,800 households across four villages","e":"Provided integrated cable TV, internet, and value-added services.","table":null},{"a":"Virtual Desktop Interface (VDI) technology","e":"","table":null},{"a":"G.O. MS No. 9 (December 2024)","e":"Over 3,500 Government institutions have already been connected under this mandate.","table":null},{"a":"1,370 centres","e":"This facilitates weekly video-based advisories benefiting over 30,000 farmers weekly (approx. 16 lakh participations annually).","table":null},{"a":"Adavi Srirampur, Peddapalli district","e":"Established at a Government school, providing rural students exposure to AI tools.","table":null},{"a":"Indian Mobile Congress 2025","e":"Endorsed by the Union Minister for Communications.","table":null},{"a":"10,000 schools","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Best Conceived Cohort 2025","e":"For the AIC T-Hub Semiconductor Program.","table":null},{"a":"Two GOLD SKOCH Awards for ADeX and VanNetra","e":"ADeX (Agriculture Data Exchange) enables secure inter-departmental data sharing; VanNetra is an AI-powered application for forest data processing.","table":null},{"a":"Agriculture Data Exchange, DigiTech Award 2025","e":"Awarded by The Economic Times under the Digital Initiative in Agriculture and Farmer Empowerment category (Jury Special Mention).","table":null},{"a":"Technology Sabha Excellence Award","e":"From the Indian Express Group, for excellence in technology-driven governance.","table":null},{"a":"National Digital Transformation Award","e":"The 19th National Digital Transformation Award 2025, under the Open Data and Transparency category.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 1,336 crore","e":"This first-of-its-kind loan addresses job creation and economic development, supporting urban, women, and rural entrepreneurs.","table":null},{"a":"USD 3 trillion GSDP","e":"The services sector is projected to contribute nearly USD 1.95 trillion GSVA as the principal driver of economic expansion.","table":null}]') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET subject_id=excluded.subject_id, chapter_number=excluded.chapter_number, title=excluded.title, answers_json=excluded.answers_json;
INSERT INTO chapter_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, chapter_number, title, answers_json) VALUES ('telangana-economy-chapter-7-infrastructure', 'telangana-economy-en', 7, 'Infrastructure', '[{"a":"23,187 MW","e":"As on 25 January 2026, up from 20,191 MW in 2023-24 and 20,884 MW in 2024-25.","table":{"headers":["Year","Contracted Capacity (MW)"],"rows":[["2023-24","20,191"],["2024-25","20,884"],["2025-26*","23,187"]],"unit":"MW; *as on 25 January 2026"}},{"a":"11.03%","e":"This was more than three times the 3.43% growth recorded between 2023-24 and 2024-25.","table":null},{"a":"27,720 MW","e":"","table":null},{"a":"55.45% (12,858 MW)","e":"The remaining 44.55% (10,329 MW) comes from renewable sources including hydro; solar has the largest renewable share.","table":null},{"a":"State Sector largest","e":"As on 25 January 2026, reflecting a diversified supply base.","table":{"headers":["Sector","Capacity (MW)","Share (%)"],"rows":[["State","11,099","47.9"],["Private","8,279","35.7"],["Central","3,809","16.4"]],"unit":"MW, as on 25 January 2026"}},{"a":"8,823 MW","e":"Largest share within the State Sector, comprising Thermal, Hydro, and Solar stations.","table":{"headers":["Source","2023-24","2024-25","2025-26 (as on 25 Jan 2026)"],"rows":[["Thermal","4,042","4,842","6,380"],["Hydro","2,442","2,442","2,442"],["Solar","1","1","1"],["Total","6,485","7,285","8,823"]],"unit":"MW"}},{"a":"Yadadri Thermal Power Station","e":"Entirely attributable to commissioning of three units of 800 MW each, out of the planned 5×800 MW supercritical project; the overall increase was 36.04%.","table":null},{"a":"2026","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Ramagundam Thermal Power Station-B (RTS-B)","e":"","table":null},{"a":"About 245 MW","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 6,604 crore","e":"To meet new emission norms notified by the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change, targeted for completion by June 2026.","table":null},{"a":"392","e":"As on 25 January 2026, along with 31,085 Circuit Kilometres of EHT lines.","table":null},{"a":"3,322","e":"As on 1 January 2026, with 7.09 lakh km length of distribution line.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 281.6 crore","e":"","table":null},{"a":"17,162 MW peak (2024-25)","e":"Peak load demand reached 17,162 MW on 20 March 2025; maximum energy consumption reached 335 MU on 18 March 2025.","table":{"headers":["Year","Peak Load Demand (MW)","Max Energy Met (MU/day)","Total Energy Supplied (MU)"],"rows":[["2022-23","15,497","298","75,956"],["2023-24","15,623","309","85,644"],["2024-25","17,162","335","88,928"]],"unit":"MW / MU/day / MU"}},{"a":"2,508 kWh","e":"Figure for 2023-24.","table":null},{"a":"Net worth rose, loans declined","e":"","table":{"headers":["Metric","Earlier Period","Later Period"],"rows":[["Net worth (Rs. crore)","2,443 (2023-24)","2,663 (2024-25)"],["Outstanding loans (Rs. crore)","10,027 (01.12.2023)","7,361 (30.11.2025)"]],"unit":"Rs. crore"}},{"a":"1.98 crore","e":"As on 1 January 2026, carried out by two DISCOMs, TGSPDCL and TGNPDCL.","table":{"headers":["Connection Type","Count"],"rows":[["Domestic","1.42 crore"],["Agricultural","30.20 lakh"],["Commercial","21.06 lakh"],["Industrial","1.58 lakh"]],"unit":"Number of connections"}},{"a":"10,329 MW","e":"As on 25 January 2026; solar has the largest share, followed by hydro.","table":{"headers":["Source","Capacity (MW)","Share (%)"],"rows":[["Solar","7,546","73.0"],["Hydro","2,521","24.4"],["Biomass/Bagasse & Waste to Energy","135","1.3"],["Wind","128","1.2"]],"unit":"MW"}},{"a":"20,000 MW by 2030, 40,000 MW by 2035","e":"","table":null},{"a":"2,042","e":"With an aggregated capacity of 58.4 MW; solarisation of another 363 buildings (10.1 MW) is in progress, and tenders are underway for 9,939 Government schools (46.7 MW proposed).","table":null},{"a":"81","e":"Covering solarisation of 40,349 domestic households and 16,840 agricultural pump sets, with a total budget of Rs. 943.47 crore.","table":null},{"a":"64 MW","e":"At 2 MW per district across 32 districts, for setting up 1 MW solar plants on Government-leased land.","table":null},{"a":"90%","e":"The remaining 10% would be cleaner thermal technologies supported by Carbon Capture, Utilisation and Storage (CCUS).","table":null},{"a":"March 2024","e":"Provides up to 200 units per month free; a valid Food Security Card, Aadhaar linkage, and domestic connection are required.","table":null},{"a":"10.97 crore zero bills","e":"Involving a total subsidy of Rs. 3,901 crore, from March 2024.","table":null},{"a":"29,677","e":"With a total subsidy of Rs. 181.68 crore, under the scheme implemented from September 2024.","table":null},{"a":"37","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Angul District, Odisha","e":"Rated capacity of 10 MTPA; inaugurated in April 2025.","table":null},{"a":"21.30 MTPA","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Singareni Thermal Power Plant (STPP)","e":"A 1×800 MW ultra-supercritical plant; SCCL also signed an MoU with RVUNL to explore a 2×800 MW pit-head plant.","table":null},{"a":"245.5 MW","e":"A further 259 MW is under construction, with 1,800 MW more at an initial proposal stage.","table":null},{"a":"Medipalli and Yellandu","e":"500 MW planned at Medipalli and 100 MW at Yellandu, using de-coaled mine areas as water reservoirs.","table":null},{"a":"Ramagundam","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Pagideru region, Manuguru","e":"","table":null},{"a":"127.69 lakh acres","e":"Through efficient, equitable, and sustainable irrigation service delivery.","table":null},{"a":"74.51 lakh acres","e":"Reflects progress in translating planned capacity into functional irrigation coverage.","table":null},{"a":"Major projects largest share","e":"Major projects account for the largest share of contemplated irrigation potential.","table":{"headers":["Category","Contemplated IP (Lakh Acres)","Share (%)"],"rows":[["Major","91.79","71.9"],["Minor","25.71","20.1"],["Small Lifts (IDC)","5.33","4.2"],["Medium","4.86","3.8"]],"unit":"Lakh Acres / %"}},{"a":"Grand total 74.51 lakh acres created","e":"","table":{"headers":["Category","Contemplated IP","Created IP","Balance IP"],"rows":[["Major Projects","91.79","40.49","51.30"],["Medium Projects","4.86","4.14","0.72"],["Minor","25.71","25.12","0.59"],["IDC","5.33","4.76","0.57"],["Grand Total","127.69","74.51","53.18"]],"unit":"Lakh Acres"}},{"a":"73 taken up, 42 completed","e":"Of 73 projects (34 Major, 39 Medium), 42 (10 Major, 32 Medium) are completed and 31 (24 Major, 7 Medium) are ongoing.","table":null},{"a":"Makthal-Narayanpet–Kodangal Lift Irrigation Scheme","e":"Includes a drinking water facility for en route villages (0.385 TMC).","table":null},{"a":"About 44,032","e":"","table":null},{"a":"5.33 lakh acres","e":"Under 557 schemes total; 647 IDC schemes exist with a net ayacut of 4.18 lakh acres, of which 53 are ongoing with a contemplated ayacut of 1.15 lakh acres.","table":null},{"a":"Sadarmatt Barrage","e":"Other Category A projects include Nilwai, Palemvagu, Mathadivagu, SRSP Stage-II, and Pipri LIS.","table":null},{"a":"More than 75% work completed","e":"Includes projects like Chinna Kaleshwaram, Sripada Yellampally, Sitarama LIS, and AMRSLBC.","table":null},{"a":"147 villages, Rs. 3,900 crore","e":"Cumulative expenditure incurred up to December 2025 is Rs. 2,750 crore, across 15 projects.","table":null},{"a":"Godavari River","e":"Also examining flood protection measures in Mancherial Town.","table":null},{"a":"Overall improvement","e":"Reflects positive changes in recharge, extractable resources, and availability for future use.","table":null},{"a":"7-9% improvement across indicators","e":"","table":{"headers":["Description","2024 (MCM)","2025 (MCM)","Improvement (%)"],"rows":[["Annual Ground Water Recharge","20,402","21,926","7"],["Annual Extractable Ground Water Resource","18,442","19,840","7"],["Groundwater Extraction for all uses","8,466","9,262","9"],["Net Annual Ground Water Available for Future Use","9,945","10,674","7"]],"unit":"Million Cubic Meters (MCM)"}},{"a":"4,57,784 dug wells; 11,21,465 tube wells","e":"","table":null},{"a":"Irrigation for tribal farming communities","e":"Under the Annual Action Plan 2025-26, it aims to create irrigation potential of 27,184 acres benefiting 10,000 tribal farming communities on Recognition of Forest Rights (RoFR) lands.","table":null},{"a":"1,29,017 km","e":"Includes 30 National Highways, reflecting extensive road coverage across the state.","table":null},{"a":"Panchayat Raj largest share","e":"","table":{"headers":["Department","Length (km)","Share (%)"],"rows":[["Panchayat Raj","68,709","53.2"],["Roads & Buildings","29,075","22.5"],["CURE","12,936","10.0"],["Other ULBs","13,314","10.3"],["National Highways","4,983","3.9"]],"unit":"km / %"}},{"a":"115.11 km per 100 sq. km","e":"The density of notified National Highways is 4.45 km per 100 sq. km.","table":null},{"a":"74.5% (92,435 km)","e":"Out of a total 1,24,034 km network excluding National Highways.","table":null},{"a":"CURE and Other ULBs","e":"","table":{"headers":["Department","All-weather roads (km)","Share of total (%)"],"rows":[["PRED Roads","38,278","55.7"],["R&B Roads","27,907","96.0"],["CURE","12,936","100.0"],["Other ULBs","13,314","100.0"],["Total","92,435","74.5"]],"unit":"km / %"}},{"a":"Rs. 11,558 crore","e":"","table":null},{"a":"861 km","e":"Against a contemplated 1,002 km, with construction expenditure of Rs. 15,564 crore.","table":null},{"a":"58 projects","e":"Covering 1,800 km at an estimated cost of Rs. 34,530 crore under State R&B (NH) wing and NHAI.","table":null},{"a":"161.52 km, Rs. 8,535 crore","e":"Notified as NH-161AA, entrusted to NHAI, planned with six-lane configuration (provision for eight lanes) under Hybrid Annuity Model; land acquisition is in progress.","table":null},{"a":"201 km","e":"Alignment passes through Choutuppal–Amangal–Sangareddy; submitted to MoRTH for approval.","table":null},{"a":"Rs. 7,327 crore","e":"Covering 253 works, 1,720 km of road length, and 41 bridge works.","table":null},{"a":"60:40 Centre-State","e":"Under PMGSY